From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cgroup: one credential rule for moving another user's task

This tree has the Android "generic cgroup subsystem permission checks":
when a writer to "tasks" or "cgroup.procs" is neither root nor the owner
of the task, attach_task_by_pid() asks every controller of the hierarchy
through an allow_attach() callback, and a controller without one means
EACCES. Only the cpu controller has a callback, and it does not check
anything:

  - attach_task_by_pid() builds the taskset for the callback with
    tset.single.cgrp = cgrp, the destination cgroup;
  - cpu_cgroup_allow_attach() walks it with
    cgroup_taskset_for_each(task, cgrp, tset), which skips every task
    whose taskset cgroup equals its second argument - the same
    destination cgroup. The loop body, the only place with a credential
    check, never runs and the function returns 0.

So the rule in this tree is all or nothing, by hierarchy:

  - cpu alone (/dev/cpuctl on Android): anybody who can open the file may
    move any task of any user. File modes and SELinux are the only limit,
    and gts2-common's init.samsung.rc made three of these files 0666.
  - any hierarchy with another controller (freezer, cpuset, cpuacct,
    memory, ...): nobody but root and the owner, which is why the cpuset
    and freezer series each had to add a callback of their own for
    Android's system_server.

Replace the callbacks with one check in attach_task_by_pid(): the writer
may move the task if it is root, if its euid is the uid or saved uid of
the task (both as before), or if it has CAP_SYS_NICE over the task's user
namespace, the capability that already lets it change the scheduling
policy and CPU affinity of other users' tasks. Remove
cgroup_subsys.allow_attach, cgroup_allow_attach(),
cpu_cgroup_allow_attach() and the paragraph in
Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt.

This is the rule later Android common kernels apply to every v1
hierarchy. According to the headers of the two patches this one replaces
(not re-checked against those trees for this patch): android-3.18
d87187392620 "CHROMIUM: cgroups: relax permissions on moving tasks
between cgroups", and cgroup_procs_write_permission() with
ns_capable(tcred->user_ns, CAP_SYS_NICE) in the 4.4 and 4.14 Android
kernels. There is no mainline commit; mainline never had allow_attach().

Effect per controller, for a writer that is not root and does not own
the task (the file's owner, mode and SELinux label apply first, as
always):

  cpu       was: always allowed.   now: CAP_SYS_NICE.
  freezer   was: EACCES.           now: CAP_SYS_NICE.
  cpuset    was: EACCES.           now: CAP_SYS_NICE.
  cpuacct, memory, blkio, devices, net_cls, net_prio, perf_event,
  hugetlb, debug, bcache
            was: EACCES.           now: CAP_SYS_NICE.
            (Of these only cpuacct is built with
            lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig, and Android does not mount it.)

This tree has no schedtune controller. No other code calls or implements
allow_attach(): the kernel-internal movers (cgroup_attach_task_all() for
vhost, cgroup_transfer_tasks() for the cpuset hotplug path) call
cgroup_attach_task() directly and never went through the check.

This patch replaces two patches of the staged series, which only added
the missing callbacks with the same CAP_SYS_NICE test:
  staging/backport-cpusets  0015 "cpuset: let CAP_SYS_NICE move other
                            users' tasks into a cpuset"
  staging/backport-freezer  0024 "cgroup: freezer: let CAP_SYS_NICE move
                            tasks of other users"

ns_capable() is evaluated last, so a writer that passes as root or owner
causes no capability check and no SELinux audit record. It is called
under rcu_read_lock() like the capable() of the old callback.

Compile-tested: zImage with the GCC 4.9 arm-linux-androideabi prebuilt,
with this patch as the first of the combined series (0014-0024), with
CONFIG_CPUSETS=y and without, and with CONFIG_CGROUP_FREEZER off; no
warnings in the files touched. Not run on the device.
---
 Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt |  9 --------
 include/linux/cgroup.h            |  1 -
 kernel/cgroup.c                   | 45 +++++++++++++---------------------------
 kernel/sched/core.c               | 18 ----------------
 4 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 59 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt b/Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt
index 61dc0ec5..638bf17f 100644
--- a/Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt
+++ b/Documentation/cgroups/cgroups.txt
@@ -598,15 +598,6 @@ is completely unused; @cgrp->parent is still valid. (Note - can also
 be called for a newly-created cgroup if an error occurs after this
 subsystem's create() method has been called for the new cgroup).
 
-int allow_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
-(cgroup_mutex held by caller)
-
-Called prior to moving a task into a cgroup; if the subsystem
-returns an error, this will abort the attach operation.  Used
-to extend the permission checks - if all subsystems in a cgroup
-return 0, the attach will be allowed to proceed, even if the
-default permission check (root or same user) fails.
-
 int can_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
 (cgroup_mutex held by caller)
 
diff --git a/include/linux/cgroup.h b/include/linux/cgroup.h
index 32a4f95d..8852d370 100644
--- a/include/linux/cgroup.h
+++ b/include/linux/cgroup.h
@@ -578,7 +578,6 @@ struct cgroup_subsys {
 	void (*css_offline)(struct cgroup *cgrp);
 	void (*css_free)(struct cgroup *cgrp);
 
-	int (*allow_attach)(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset);
 	int (*can_attach)(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset);
 	void (*cancel_attach)(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset);
 	void (*attach)(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset);
diff --git a/kernel/cgroup.c b/kernel/cgroup.c
index 9f0fb7e6..62e5a68d 100644
--- a/kernel/cgroup.c
+++ b/kernel/cgroup.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
  *  distribution for more details.
  */
 
+#include <linux/capability.h>
 #include <linux/cgroup.h>
 #include <linux/cred.h>
 #include <linux/ctype.h>
@@ -2112,24 +2113,6 @@ out_free_group_list:
 	return retval;
 }
 
-static int cgroup_allow_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
-{
-	struct cgroup_subsys *ss;
-	int ret;
-
-	for_each_subsys(cgrp->root, ss) {
-		if (ss->allow_attach) {
-			ret = ss->allow_attach(cgrp, tset);
-			if (ret)
-				return ret;
-		} else {
-			return -EACCES;
-		}
-	}
-
-	return 0;
-}
-
 /*
  * Find the task_struct of the task to attach by vpid and pass it along to the
  * function to attach either it or all tasks in its threadgroup. Will lock
@@ -2156,23 +2139,23 @@ retry_find_task:
 		/*
 		 * even if we're attaching all tasks in the thread group, we
 		 * only need to check permissions on one of them.
+		 *
+		 * The writer may move the task if it is root, if it owns the
+		 * task, or if it has CAP_SYS_NICE, the capability that already
+		 * lets it change the scheduling policy and CPU affinity of
+		 * other users' tasks.  Android's system_server (uid system)
+		 * moves the processes of every app between the cgroups of the
+		 * cpu, cpuset and freezer controllers that way.  The rule is
+		 * the same for every hierarchy, whatever controllers it has.
 		 */
 		tcred = __task_cred(tsk);
 		if (!uid_eq(cred->euid, GLOBAL_ROOT_UID) &&
 		    !uid_eq(cred->euid, tcred->uid) &&
-		    !uid_eq(cred->euid, tcred->suid)) {
-			/*
-			 * if the default permission check fails, give each
-			 * cgroup a chance to extend the permission check
-			 */
-			struct cgroup_taskset tset = { };
-			tset.single.task = tsk;
-			tset.single.cgrp = cgrp;
-			ret = cgroup_allow_attach(cgrp, &tset);
-			if (ret) {
-				rcu_read_unlock();
-				goto out_unlock_cgroup;
-			}
+		    !uid_eq(cred->euid, tcred->suid) &&
+		    !ns_capable(tcred->user_ns, CAP_SYS_NICE)) {
+			rcu_read_unlock();
+			ret = -EACCES;
+			goto out_unlock_cgroup;
 		}
 	} else
 		tsk = current;
diff --git a/kernel/sched/core.c b/kernel/sched/core.c
index 10530390..3f48a4c0 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/core.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/core.c
@@ -7958,23 +7958,6 @@ static void cpu_cgroup_css_offline(struct cgroup *cgrp)
 	sched_offline_group(tg);
 }
 
-static int
-cpu_cgroup_allow_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
-{
-	const struct cred *cred = current_cred(), *tcred;
-	struct task_struct *task;
-
-	cgroup_taskset_for_each(task, cgrp, tset) {
-		tcred = __task_cred(task);
-
-		if ((current != task) && !capable(CAP_SYS_NICE) &&
-		    cred->euid != tcred->uid && cred->euid != tcred->suid)
-			return -EACCES;
-	}
-
-	return 0;
-}
-
 static int cpu_cgroup_can_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp,
 				 struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
 {
@@ -8341,7 +8324,6 @@ struct cgroup_subsys cpu_cgroup_subsys = {
 	.css_offline	= cpu_cgroup_css_offline,
 	.can_attach	= cpu_cgroup_can_attach,
 	.attach		= cpu_cgroup_attach,
-	.allow_attach	= cpu_cgroup_allow_attach,
 	.exit		= cpu_cgroup_exit,
 	.subsys_id	= cpu_cgroup_subsys_id,
 	.base_cftypes	= cpu_files,
